0référence et bibliographie0111134010
0 Note : A Lepista specie will be recognizable as such in the field as a tricholomatoid or clitocyboid agaric with gills that separate easily from cap and stem. A set of colours, odours  and growth habits further helps to characterize the lepistas. The feature of the separating gills was crucial for FRIES (1836) when he separated genus Paxillus (of which tribus Lepista became part in 1836-38) from Agaricus and for MAIRE (1913) when he errected Rhodopaxillus to accomodate L. nuda, L. personata and related species.011213500
0 Cap pale, almost white from a ± canescent surface, or pinkish brown as moist and whitish when dry, gills crowded011313602
0 *. Cap brown in moist and dry condition, gills close011413703
0 Gills deeply decurrent and attenuated on stem, flesh ± dis- colouring reddish, cap not hygrophanous, spores 4-4.5 x 3-3.5 µm 01151380 L. densifolia 10
0 *. Gills rounded at the stem, becoming somewhat decurrent, flesh not discolouring, cap hygrophanous, spores 4.5-5.8(-7)x 3-4(-4.5) µm 02361490 L. subconnexa  50
0 Pigments encrusting, in places seen as transverse lines on the hyphal wall in pileipellis0237141004
0 *. Pigments not encrusting0348151105
0 Habit slender, cap soon flaccid and flexuous, stem soon hollow, spores 5.3-7(-8.5)x3.5-4.5 µm, in grassy habitats, often lawns 034915120 L. ovispora 30
0 *. Habit robust, cap fleshy, stem stuffed, finally hollow, spores 6-7.5(-9)x3.5-5 µm, in disturbed habitats such as construction sites and peat cracks, arctic-alpine 0471016130 L. multiforme  20
0 Dry cap usually with patches of white tomentum-like crystals, spores 4.5-6.5x3-3.8 µm, arctic-alpine 0581117140  L. pseudoectypa  40
0 Dry cap naked, spores 6-8x4-5 µm, Californa 0581217150 L. harperi0
